WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.227
Assumptions are beliefs or conditions that reasoning depends on.

2
00:00:04.227 --> 00:00:08.303
Some are explicit, such as assuming a budget limit or a deadline.

3
00:00:08.303 --> 00:00:17.662
Others are hidden, such as assuming users will behave a certain way, a data source is complete, or a team will adopt a new process without resistance.

4
00:00:17.662 --> 00:00:23.096
Critical thinking asks what are we assuming before the group commits to a conclusion.

5
00:00:23.096 --> 00:00:29.059
Problem framing determines what information is noticed and what solutions seem reasonable.

6
00:00:29.059 --> 00:00:35.777
If a system outage is framed only as an engineering mistake, the team may focus on individual performance.

7
00:00:35.777 --> 00:00:43.551
If it is framed as a resilience problem, the team may examine architecture, monitoring, handoff, runbooks, and workload.

8
00:00:43.551 --> 00:00:48.155
The frame does not decide the answer, but it shapes where attention goes.

9
00:00:48.155 --> 00:00:50.872
Scope and definitions are part of framing.

10
00:00:50.872 --> 00:01:02.194
A team discussing secure design needs to agree whether it means code quality, identity controls, threat modeling, supply chain risk, deployment configuration, or all of those.

11
00:01:02.194 --> 00:01:08.383
Without shared definitions, people may appear aligned while defending different conclusions.

12
00:01:08.383 --> 00:01:13.365
Clear definitions reduce false agreement and make disagreement more useful.

13
00:01:13.365 --> 00:01:16.158
Context can change the meaning of evidence.

14
00:01:16.158 --> 00:01:20.233
A process that works in a small team may fail at enterprise scale.

15
00:01:20.233 --> 00:01:26.121
A control that is reasonable in a low-risk environment may be inadequate for regulated data.

16
00:01:26.121 --> 00:01:32.989
Incentives, stakeholders, constraints, history, and timing all affect what counts as a practical answer.

17
00:01:32.989 --> 00:01:38.499
Critical thinkers look at the environment around the claim, not only the claim itself.

18
00:01:38.499 --> 00:01:41.820
Before committing, ask several framing questions.

19
00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.688
What problem are we actually trying to solve?

20
00:01:44.688 --> 00:01:46.802
What is inside and outside scope?

21
00:01:46.802 --> 00:01:48.009
Who is affected?

22
00:01:48.009 --> 00:01:49.821
What incentives are at work?

23
00:01:49.821 --> 00:01:52.312
What would change our interpretation?

24
00:01:52.312 --> 00:02:00.539
These questions help prevent a team from solving a simplified version of the problem while the real conditions remain untouched.
